-- title:
--   Convolution pⁿ-th power kills coordinates of Witt homomorphisms
-- statement:
--   Let $k$ be a field of characteristic $p$ for a prime $p$, let $n \geq 1$, and let $A$ be a commutative ring carrying the structure of a Hopf algebra over $k$ which is finite-dimensional as a $k$-module and whose comultiplication is cocommutative. Let $\beta$ be an element of the convolution monoid `WithConv` on the $k$-linear dual $A \to_{\ell} k$, with underlying linear functional $\beta$`.ofConv`, and assume this functional vanishes at $1 \in A$. Let $s$ belong to the $k$-subalgebra `Algebra.adjoin` of $A$ generated by the set of all $a \in A$ that occur as the $j$-th Witt coordinate, for some index $j \in$ `Fin n`, of some element $x$ of [`Deformation.wittHom k p n A`](def/Dieudonne_WittVectorHom.html#L246), the additive subgroup of the truncated Witt vectors `TruncatedWittVector p n A` of length $n$ over $A$ consisting of those $x$ for which the image of $x$ under the Witt functor applied to the comultiplication $A \to A \otimes_k A$ equals the sum of its images under the two inclusions $A \to A \otimes_k A$ on the left and on the right. Then the $p^n$-th power of $\beta$ in the convolution monoid, viewed as a linear functional, vanishes at $s$: $(\beta^{p^n})$`.ofConv` $s = 0$.
--
--   The elements of [`Deformation.wittHom k p n A`](def/Dieudonne_WittVectorHom.html#L246) are the primitive truncated Witt vectors over $A$, that is, the homomorphisms from the finite group scheme with coordinate ring $A$ to the group of Witt vectors of length $n$; the subalgebra generated by their coordinates is the coordinate ring of the image of that group scheme in a product of copies of $W_n$. Since convolution powers by a functional killing $1$ compute the transpose of the Verschiebung on the Cartier dual, the statement expresses that such an image is killed by $V^n$, as $W_n$ is; it is used in the proof of [`HopfAlgebra.wittHomMap_surjective_of_surjective_of_wittHomShift_surjective`](thm.html#HopfAlgebra.wittHomMap_surjective_of_surjective_of_wittHomShift_surjective), and the argument cites [`Deformation.convPow_prime_apply_coeff_of_mem_wittHom`](thm.html#Deformation.convPow_prime_apply_coeff_of_mem_wittHom), which identifies the $p$-th convolution power on Witt coordinates with the shift-and-Frobenius recursion.
